<div class="qul-docs">
  <p>
    A <strong>stem</strong> sits between the root and the word as it actually appears. It is the base
      pattern a word carries before prefixes, suffixes and inflection are applied, which makes it useful for
      grouping closely related surface forms.
  </p>

  <div class="docs-callout docs-callout-info">
    This resource gives the stems of an <strong>ayah at a time</strong>: for each ayah, the stem of each of
      its words in order. If you need the stem of one specific word instead, use the
      <a href="/resources/morphology">Word stem</a> resource.
  </div>

  <h2>Example</h2>
  <p>Ayah <code>73:4</code> has these stems:</p>
  <div class="docs-code">
    <div class="docs-code-header"><span>73:4</span></div>
    <pre><code class="qpc-hafs">أَوْ زِدْ عَلَيْ رَتِّلِ قُرْءَانَ تَرْتِيلًا</code></pre>
  </div>

  <h2>Export format</h2>
  <p>
    QUL exports this data as a SQLite database with one row per ayah.
  </p>
  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr>
        <th>Column</th>
        <th>Type</th>
        <th>What it holds</th>
      </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr>
        <td><code>ayah_key</code></td>
        <td>TEXT</td>
        <td>The ayah, in <code>surah:ayah</code> form — for example <code>73:4</code>.</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><code>text</code></td>
        <td>TEXT</td>
        <td>The stem of each word in the ayah, in word order, separated by spaces.</td>
      </tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <div class="docs-callout docs-callout-warning">
    The stems are listed in word order but are not individually numbered. To line them up with the words
    of the ayah you also need the word by word Quran script, available on the
    <a href="/resources/quran-script">Quran Scripts</a> page.
  </div>
</div>
